; ======================================================================
;  THE MEMORY MAP - COMPUTED FROM THE IMAGE, NOT TYPED AS A WINDOW
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RULING 2026-09-08: "the bootloader is a kernel and can be as big as it
;  wants." This file used to answer the question "how big may the monitor
;  be" with a constant - #MON_HI = $003FFFFF, a two-megabyte window - and
;  everything else in the map was positioned around that constant. That
;  is a SIZE CAP wearing a map's clothes, and by 2026-09-08 it was 16,872
;  bytes from binding: the image was 2,080,024 bytes of a 2,096,896-byte
;  budget.
;
;  A kernel does not work that way and this one no longer does. The
;  toolchain marks the end of the image with a linker symbol - the same
;  thing Linux calls `_end` (arch/arm64/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S) and reserves
;  `_text.._end` from at run time - and RaspberryPi4/EmitCore_A64.pbi
;  emits __image_start__ / __image_end__ on every A64 build. The pair
;  costs zero bytes; an image built before and after that change hashes
;  the same. What it buys is that THE RUNNING PROGRAM CAN MEASURE ITSELF,
;  so the map below is arithmetic on the image rather than a set of
;  numbers somebody has to remember to raise.
;
;  WHAT IS STILL A CONSTANT, AND WHY EACH ONE IS A FACT RATHER THAN A CAP:
;
;    #MON_LO       the address the FIRMWARE loads kernel8.img to. It is
;                  CONFIG.TXT's kernel_address on the stick and the build
;                  line's LoadAddress, and it is not this program's
;                  choice. A fact about the boot path.
;    #MON_STACK    the top of the monitor's own stack, moved there by
;                  three instructions at the top of Main(). Below the
;                  image on purpose; see THE STACK in board.pi4.
;    #AB_BASE      the autoboot record. A FIXED page that no image size
;                  can reach - see THE AUTOBOOT RECORD below, which is
;                  where it moved to on 2026-09-08 and why.
;    #MON_DATA_*   where --bss-addr puts this program's variables. A
;                  placement decision, not a budget; the 8 MiB size is
;                  what HitsMonitor() refuses and is argued below.
;    #PAY0_HI      the top of the low payload window - one clear megabyte
;                  below the default payload stack top.
;    #PAY1_LO/HI   the whole of DRAM bank 1, measured from bdinfo.
;
;  EVERYTHING ELSE IS COMPUTED: where the monitor's reserved region ends,
;  where the low payload window begins, and where a host is told to stage
;  a file. All three move when the image does, in one place, and the
;  `map` command prints them with the byte count they came from.
;
;  THE GRAIN IS ONE MEGABYTE AND THAT IS THE GUARD. The monitor's reserved
;  region is rounded UP to a whole megabyte, and the low payload window
;  starts at the megabyte boundary above the image. So a payload address
;  stays valid until the monitor crosses a megabyte boundary, and on the
;  day it does the board says so in a full sentence with the new window in
;  it rather than letting an image land on the monitor. A finer grain
;  would move payload addresses on almost every build; a coarser one
;  would hand back megabytes of DRAM for nothing.
;
;  WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN the image was 2,080,024 bytes and the
;  arithmetic produced $00200000..$003FFFFF and $00400000..$07EFFFFF -
;  the two constants this file used to carry, derived instead of typed,
;  which is a reassuring result and a weak proof. TODAY IT IS 2,107,672
;  BYTES, ending at $00402917, so it produces $00200000..$004FFFFF and
;  $00500000..$07EFFFFF and the reassuring coincidence is over. Do not
;  update this paragraph with a third number: type `map`, which is the
;  whole point of it.
; ======================================================================

#MON_STACK = $00100000         ; monitor stack top, grows down
#MON_LO    = $00200000         ; monitor image base (--load-addr,
                               ; CONFIG.TXT kernel_address)

; The granularity the map moves in. See THE GRAIN IS ONE MEGABYTE above.
#MON_GRAIN = $00100000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE AUTOBOOT RECORD - MOVED OUT OF THE IMAGE WINDOW 2026-09-08
;
;  IT WAS AT $003FFF00, the last 256 bytes of the old two-megabyte
;  window, and that was the CEILING: the record was the first thing a
;  growing image would land on, and nothing about a fixed record inside a
;  region whose size is the image's is repairable. It had to leave.
;
;  WHERE IT WENT AND WHY THAT PAGE. $001FF000 is the last page below
;  #MON_LO. Four things are true of it and all four are required:
;
;    no image size can reach it   the image grows UP from #MON_LO. There
;                                 is no number of bytes of code that
;                                 arrives at an address below its own
;                                 base.
;    the stack cannot reach it    #MON_STACK is $00100000 and the monitor's
;                                 stack grows DOWN from there, into
;                                 $000FFFFF and below. This page is a
;                                 megabyte ABOVE the stack top, in the
;                                 band between the stack and the image
;                                 that nothing else claims.
;    _start cannot erase it       it is not BSS. The zeroing loop walks
;                                 __bss_start__..__bss_end__, which
;                                 --bss-addr puts at $08200000. This was
;                                 the SILENT failure the old address had
;                                 and it is now impossible twice over.
;    its address does not move    which is the whole requirement a record
;                                 read after a reset by a possibly
;                                 REBUILT image has, and it is why the
;                                 record cannot be a NoClear Global: a
;                                 NoClear Global's address moves when
;                                 anything above it changes size.
;
;  THE 2026-08-28 NOTE SAID THERE WAS NOWHERE TO PUT IT and that moving it
;  bought 256 bytes. Both statements were answers to a different question.
;  "Nowhere to put it" was about a WATCHDOG SCRATCH REGISTER - a place
;  that survives a reset of the SoC - and that is still true: this part
;  has none this project can cite (Lib/safety.pi4:107-112). The record
;  never needed one. It needs raw DRAM at a fixed address that survives
;  _start, and DRAM below the load address is exactly that, which is
;  where #SAFETY_COUNT_ADDR already lives at $00001000. And "it buys 256
;  bytes" was true while the ceiling was #AB_BASE; with the ceiling gone
;  the record is not buying space, it is getting out of the way of a
;  region that now has no top.
;
;  IT IS STILL PROTECTED. It is outside both payload windows, so `b`, `l`,
;  `load` and `get` refuse it through InPayload(); `w`, `g`, `a`, `fill`
;  and `copy` are not window-checked, so it is declared below as one of
;  the regions HwMonRegion*() names and HitsMonitor() refuses. That is the
;  same reasoning that added the data window as a second region in
;  2026-08-28 - a region the monitor owns that no window test covers.
;
;  THE DSI FRAMEBUFFER IS THE FOURTH REGION. Main added its fixed 8 MiB
;  allocation after the no-size-cap topic branched, so the topic's list of
;  three could not name it. It is monitor-owned DMA/scanout memory outside
;  both payload windows and therefore belongs in the same board-declared
;  refusal list as code, variables and the autoboot page.
;
;  NOTHING READS IT BEFORE THE IMAGE RUNS. The firmware loads kernel8.img
;  and branches; it has never been told this address exists. The only
;  readers are AutoRead() and AutoFlush() in RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4,
;  called from this monitor's own boot path (Board/boot.pi4) after
;  _start, so moving the address is a change to one file's constant and
;  to nothing outside this image.
;
;  ALIGNMENT IS UNCHANGED IN KIND. $001FF000 is 4 KiB aligned and
;  therefore 64-byte aligned, so AutoFlush() still needs exactly one
;  cache line for all three words ($1FF000..$1FF017).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#AB_BASE   = $001FF000
#AB_BYTES  = $00001000         ; the whole page is reserved, not 24 bytes:
                               ; a refusal that names a page is one an
                               ; operator can act on, and the page is not
                               ; wanted for anything else.
#AB_MAGIC  = $5041594C4F414421 ; "PAYLOAD!" - positive in a signed .i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RAW SECONDARY STACK RESERVATION - INACTIVE, BOARD OWNED
;
;  Cores 1..3 each own one exact 4 KiB page immediately below the autoboot
;  page. The whole $001FC000..$001FEFFF band is below the fixed firmware load
;  address, above the primary stack top, outside BSS, framebuffer and both
;  payload windows, and cannot move when the linked image grows upward.
;  core_worker.pi4 is not in the live include graph; these accessors establish
;  ownership and monitor protection without activating a secondary core.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CORE_RAW_STACK_LO    = $001FC000
#CORE_RAW_STACK_PAGE  = $00001000
#CORE_RAW_STACK_HI    = $001FEFFF

Procedure.i HwCoreRawStackBase(core.i)
  Select core
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #CORE_RAW_STACK_LO
    Case 2 : ProcedureReturn #CORE_RAW_STACK_LO + #CORE_RAW_STACK_PAGE
    Case 3 : ProcedureReturn #CORE_RAW_STACK_LO + (#CORE_RAW_STACK_PAGE * 2)
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwCoreRawStackBytes(core.i)
  If core >= 1 And core <= 3
    ProcedureReturn #CORE_RAW_STACK_PAGE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE MONITOR'S DATA WINDOW - the Globals, put there by --bss-addr.
;
;  UNCHANGED 2026-09-08 except that it is now one of the regions the
;  board DECLARES rather than a pair of constants the core reads. The
;  four requirements that chose $08200000 are unchanged and they are:
;
;    it must be present on every Pi 4      - so not bank 1 ($40000000
;                                            up), which a 1 GB or 2 GB
;                                            board does not have. This
;                                            program is the recovery
;                                            path; it may not depend on
;                                            the board being the big one.
;    it must not be in a payload window    - or a payload would land on
;                                            the monitor's variables and
;                                            the loader would allow it.
;    it must be above the payload's stack  - which grows DOWN from
;                                            $08000000 into $07F00000
;                                            (#A64_STACK_TOP_DEFAULT,
;                                            RaspberryPi4/A64Assembler.pbi:140)
;    it must not be #ETH_RX_BASE           - the GENET receive ring is
;                                            $08100000..$0817FFFF and a
;                                            bus master writes into it
;                                            on its own schedule.
;
;  $08180000 is the first byte clear of all four; $08200000 is the next
;  round megabyte, and the half-megabyte of daylight is so the ring can
;  double without this constant moving.
;
;  THE SIZE IS 8 MiB AND IT IS BOUNDED ON PURPOSE. It is bounded rather
;  than open-ended because #MON_DATA_HI is what HitsMonitor() refuses
;  writes into, and a refusal covering 816 MiB of memory nobody is using
;  would be a monitor forbidding most of the board on the grounds that it
;  might one day want it. If BSS ever passes 8 MiB the build does not
;  break silently - tools/a64/a64_anvil_check.py reads these two
;  constants out of this file and checks the measured BSS against them.
;
;  IT IS NOT COMPUTED FROM THE IMAGE and it does not need to be. BSS is
;  not in the image, so no image size can reach it, and the gate above
;  catches the one thing that could go wrong.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MON_DATA_LO = $08200000       ; the monitor's Globals - see --bss-addr
#MON_DATA_HI = $089FFFFF       ; on the build line at the top of board.pi4

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PAYLOAD WINDOWS. The LOW window's bottom edge is computed - see
;  HwPayLo() below. The other three edges are measured facts about this
;  board's DRAM and stay constants:
;
;    #PAY0_HI   one clear megabyte below $08000000, the stack top every
;               image this compiler emits uses unless it was built with
;               --stack-addr. A payload loaded at $07F80000 would be
;               eaten by its own first push.
;    #PAY1_*    the whole of bdinfo's second bank, which nothing else on
;               this board enters.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PAY0_HI   = $07EFFFFF
#PAY1_LO   = $40000000
#PAY1_HI   = $FBFFFFFF


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DSI PANEL'S FRAMEBUFFER - NEW 2026-09-08.
;
;  WHY THE MONITOR OWNS THIS MEMORY AT ALL, WHICH IT DOES NOT FOR HDMI.
;  On HDMI the VideoCore firmware allocates the surface and hands back an
;  address; there is nothing to place. On DSI the firmware allocates
;  NOTHING - it never brings DSI1 up (its power domain is off at boot),
;  owns no DSI display, and `screen displays` truthfully reports zero
;  even with the panel lit. Anvil composes that surface itself: it points
;  an HVS display list at these bytes and pixel valve 1 at the panel. So
;  the address is ours to choose, and a chosen address in this program is
;  A NAMED CONSTANT WITH ITS REASONS and never "the obvious next free
;  megabyte" - the same rule #MON_DATA_LO above is written under, and for
;  the same reason.
;
;  FIVE REQUIREMENTS, AND ONE BAND SATISFIES ALL FIVE:
;
;    below $40000000                 - the HVS's window on DRAM is the
;                                      FIRST GIGABYTE ONLY
;                                      (bcm2711.dtsi:45, #HVS_PHYS_MAX in
;                                      hvs.pi4). A display list pointing
;                                      above it is read without complaint
;                                      and scans out whatever is 3 GB away.
;    present on every Pi 4           - so not bank 1; this is the recovery
;                                      path and may not need the big board.
;    outside BOTH payload windows    - or a `load`, a `net recv` or a
;                                      staged upload would land on the
;                                      picture, and InPayload() would
;                                      ALLOW it, because those two windows
;                                      are the whole of what it calls
;                                      allowed. #PAY0 ends $07EFFFFF and
;                                      #PAY1 starts $40000000.
;    clear of the monitor's own text - not $00200000..the image end, and
;      AND of where tools stage        NOT $00400000 or $00800000, which
;                                      are where the board tools stage an
;                                      upload through DRAM.
;    clear of #MON_DATA and #ETH_RX  - the Globals, and the GENET receive
;                                      ring a bus master writes into on
;                                      its own schedule.
;
;  $08A00000 is the first round megabyte above #MON_DATA_HI, so it sits
;  in the band the memory map calls "free, but DELIBERATELY NOT OFFERED"
;  - beside the monitor's own variables, inside the HVS's gigabyte, and
;  outside everything a payload or a tool can reach.
;
;  THE SIZE IS 8 MiB AND IT IS TWO SCREENS, NOT ONE. 800 x 1280 x 4 is
;  4,096,000 bytes ($3E8000), so the scanout buffer runs $08A00000 ..
;  $08DE7FFF. The second buffer is what a 90 or 270 degree rotation
;  needs: the panel scans 800x1280 portrait whatever the console does,
;  so a landscape console draws into a 1280x800 buffer of its own and
;  that is turned into the portrait one on present. A transpose does not
;  change the pixel count, so the second buffer is the same 4,096,000
;  bytes; it starts at the next round megabyte after the first ends and
;  the pair fit inside 8 MiB with 96 KiB to spare.
;
;  At rotation 0 and 180 THERE IS NO SECOND BUFFER IN USE - the console
;  draws straight into the scanout one and #MON_FB_DRAW is untouched
;  memory. 180 costs nothing either, because the HVS composes it from
;  its two mirror bits; only 90 and 270 pay for the transpose.
;
;  THE PANEL IS 800x1280 AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: on this board
;  PV_HORZB read 00CF0320 and PV_VERTB read 00140500, and $320 is 800
;  with $500 for 1280.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MON_FB_LO      = $08A00000    ; the DSI framebuffer window, 8 MiB
#MON_FB_HI      = $091FFFFF
#MON_FB_SCAN    = $08A00000    ; what the HVS scans out - always the
                               ; panel's native 800 x 1280 portrait
#MON_FB_DRAW    = $08E00000    ; what the console draws into when the
                               ; rotation makes the two differ. The scan
                               ; buffer ends $08DE7FFF; this is the next
                               ; round megabyte above it.
#MON_FB_PANEL_W = 800          ; the panel's own scan geometry
#MON_FB_PANEL_H = 1280
#MON_FB_BYTES   = 4096000      ; 800 * 1280 * 4, one screen

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DRIVER-MODULE REGION - NEW 2026-09-10.
;
;  WHERE A LOADED DRIVER LIVES, and one 8 MiB region holding two windows:
;  the ARENA, where verified modules are placed and executed, and the
;  STAGING window, where a container is read to and judged before any of
;  it is believed. They are separate because the digest covers the
;  header, and the relocation table and the compatible ids sit AFTER the
;  image - so the whole file has to be in one place before any of it can
;  be trusted, and reading it into the arena first would mean writing
;  unverified bytes into the range live drivers occupy.
;
;  THE ADDRESS IN THE 2026-09-06 DESIGN NOTE IS FORBIDDEN AND THAT IS THE
;  FIRST THING TO SAY. It proposed $08A00000..$091FFFFF, argued four
;  ways, and every one of those arguments was true when it was written.
;  Two days later main allocated exactly that range for the DSI
;  framebuffer (#MON_FB_LO above), for four nearly identical reasons - it
;  is the first round megabyte above the monitor's own variables, inside
;  the HVS's gigabyte, outside both payload windows. A module loaded
;  there would be scanned out as pixels and then branched into. The
;  proposal is struck, the reason is kept here rather than deleted, and
;  ModArenaInit() now CHECKS the arena against this file's own region
;  list rather than trusting a paragraph - because the paragraph was
;  right and went stale anyway.
;
;  FIVE REQUIREMENTS, THE SAME FIVE #MON_FB_LO IS ARGUED UNDER:
;
;    present on every Pi 4          - so not bank 1 ($40000000 up), which
;                                     a 1 GB board does not have. This is
;                                     the recovery path; it may not need
;                                     the big board.
;    outside BOTH payload windows   - or a `load`, a `net recv` or a
;                                     staged upload would land on a
;                                     loaded driver and InPayload() would
;                                     ALLOW it. #PAY0 ends $07EFFFFF and
;                                     #PAY1 starts $40000000.
;    above the payload's own stack  - which grows DOWN from $08000000
;    clear of every other owner     - the Globals ($08200000..$089FFFFF),
;                                     the GENET receive ring
;                                     ($08100000..$0817FFFF, written by a
;                                     bus master on its own schedule),
;                                     the DSI framebuffer
;                                     ($08A00000..$091FFFFF), and the V3D
;                                     engine's arena ($0A000000, 16 MiB,
;                                     RaspberryPi4/Board/v3d_console.pi4)
;    a round megabyte               - so the refusal reads as a window
;
;  $09200000 IS THE FIRST ROUND MEGABYTE ABOVE THE FRAMEBUFFER, and the
;  8 MiB it takes ends at $099FFFFF - six clear megabytes below the V3D
;  arena, in the band this map calls "free, but DELIBERATELY NOT
;  OFFERED". The largest driver anybody has proposed converting is about
;  600 KiB, so six megabytes of arena holds every module this board is
;  likely to carry several times over.
;
;  THE SPLIT IS 6 MiB ARENA AND 2 MiB STAGING. Staging is a cap on how
;  big a single container this board will read, and two megabytes is
;  three times the largest driver in the tree.
;
;  IT IS DECLARED AS A MONITOR REGION, which is what protects it: `w`,
;  `fill` and `copy` are not window-checked and go through
;  HitsMonitor(), so a typo cannot scribble on a running driver. That
;  matters more here than for the framebuffer - a corrupted picture is
;  visible and a corrupted driver is a wild branch later with no message.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MOD_REGION_LO   = $09200000   ; the whole module region, 8 MiB
#MOD_REGION_HI   = $099FFFFF
#MOD_ARENA_LO    = $09200000   ; where verified modules are placed
#MOD_ARENA_BYTES = $00600000   ; 6 MiB
#MOD_STAGE_LO    = $09800000   ; where one container is read and judged
#MOD_STAGE_BYTES = $00200000   ; 2 MiB - the largest container accepted

; The index this region has in HwMonRegion*(). ModArenaInit() skips it
; when it checks the arena against the rest of the map, because a range
; necessarily overlaps itself; every other region is a real clash.
#MON_REGION_MODULES = 5

; ======================================================================
;  MonImage*() - THE IMAGE MEASURES ITSELF
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  __image_start__ and __image_end__ are emitted by
;  RaspberryPi4/EmitCore_A64.pbi on every A64 build: the first is at the
;  load base, the second is one past the last byte the firmware copies,
;  because it is emitted immediately before .bss and BSS reserves space
;  without writing bytes. adrp+add is the architecture's own way to reach
;  a symbol from anywhere in +/-4 GB, and it is what _start already uses
;  for __bss_start__.
;
;  WHY THE ANSWER GOES THROUGH FILE-SCOPE STORAGE. It used to go through
;  a procedure local, because on this backend a local was a static slot in
;  BSS and had an address the assembler could name. THAT IS NO LONGER TRUE,
;  and the change is right: as of 2026-09-11 automatic storage lives in the
;  invocation's frame, so a name for it would mean a different place on
;  every call, and the compiler refuses inline assembly that takes one as
;  an absolute symbol. The two slots below are therefore file-scope, which
;  is the layer that can honestly own an address.
;
;  THE VALUE IS STILL UNCACHEABLE BY CONSTRUCTION. Each slot is written by
;  its ASM block and read on the very next line, and nothing in the
;  procedure reads it beforehand, so the emitter's register map holds
;  nothing for it and the read must come from memory. That was the point of
;  the local and it survives the move; what would break it is a read
;  EARLIER in the same procedure, and there is none.
;
;  EACH PROCEDURE HAS ITS OWN SLOT. They hold link-time constants - the
;  same bytes on every call - so two cores asking at once cannot disagree,
;  and separate slots keep that true without either procedure having to
;  know about the other.
;
;  x16 and x17 are the scratch pair the emitted preamble itself uses for
;  the stack switch and the FP gate; A64X() hands out x9..x13 for IR
;  temporaries, so neither can be live across an ASM block here.
; ======================================================================
Global monimage_lo.i = 0       ; written by MonImageLo()'s ASM, read next line
Global monimage_end.i = 0      ; written by MonImageEnd()'s ASM, read next line

Procedure.i MonImageLo()
  ASM
    adrp x16, __image_start__
    add  x16, x16, #:lo12:__image_start__
    adrp x17, global_monimage_lo
    add  x17, x17, #:lo12:global_monimage_lo
    str  x16, [x17]
  EndASM
  ProcedureReturn monimage_lo
EndProcedure

; ONE PAST THE LAST BYTE OF THE IMAGE. Not the last byte - this is the
; end the linker states, and the arithmetic below is written against a
; half-open range for the same reason the assembler's own codeEnd is.
Procedure.i MonImageEnd()
  ASM
    adrp x16, __image_end__
    add  x16, x16, #:lo12:__image_end__
    adrp x17, global_monimage_end
    add  x17, x17, #:lo12:global_monimage_end
    str  x16, [x17]
  EndASM
  ProcedureReturn monimage_end
EndProcedure

; HOW BIG THIS MONITOR IS, in bytes, measured rather than recorded. It is
; exactly the size of the file the firmware copied: the `map` command
; prints it, and tools/a64/a64_anvil_check.py compares it against the
; length of the .img on disk, so the image and the number it reports
; about itself cannot drift.
Procedure.i HwMonBytes()
  ProcedureReturn MonImageEnd() - MonImageLo()
EndProcedure

; Round UP to the next multiple of #MON_GRAIN. Written with a divide
; rather than a mask because every address it is ever handed is a
; positive .i well inside 63 bits, and the divide says what it means.
Procedure.i MonGrainUp(a.i)
  ProcedureReturn ((a + #MON_GRAIN - 1) / #MON_GRAIN) * #MON_GRAIN
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  HwMonLo() / HwMonHi() - WHERE THIS MONITOR'S CODE ACTUALLY IS.
;
;  THEY ARE NOT CONSTANTS ANY MORE, and the difference is not cosmetic.
;  The old pair answered "$00200000..$003FFFFF" whatever image was
;  running, which meant two wrong answers at once whenever the running
;  image was not the canonical one:
;
;    a monitor built --load-addr 0x2000000 for a live-bench swap - the
;    documented way to try a new monitor without a reset - refused loads
;    into $200000..$3FFFFF, where nothing of it lived, and DID NOT refuse
;    a load on top of itself. board.pi4 recorded that as a known defect
;    and said the fix "would need the running base at run time, and this
;    file does not know it". It does now.
;
;    an image larger than two megabytes had part of itself outside the
;    region the monitor was protecting.
;
;  Both are the same defect: a region that describes an intention instead
;  of the program. These two now describe the program.
;
;  THE REGION IS ROUNDED UP TO #MON_GRAIN. HwMonHi() is the last byte of
;  the megabyte the image ends in, not the last byte of the image. See
;  THE GRAIN IS ONE MEGABYTE at the top: the rounding is what keeps a
;  payload address stable across builds, and the slack it reserves is
;  memory nothing else was offered anyway.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwMonLo()
  ProcedureReturn MonImageLo()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonHi()
  ProcedureReturn MonGrainUp(MonImageEnd()) - 1
EndProcedure

; The top of the monitor's own stack, for the paragraphs in `help` and
; the banner that describe what the monitor occupies. It is asked for
; rather than read as a constant for the same reason the pair above is:
; the core prints one sentence and every board fills it in.
Procedure.i HwMonStack()
  ProcedureReturn #MON_STACK
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  HwMonRegion*() - EVERY REGION THIS MONITOR OWNS, ENUMERATED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Anvil/Core/memrange.pbi's HitsMonitor() used to test two hard-written
;  ranges: the image window and the data window. Two was already one more
;  than it started with - the data window was added on 2026-08-28 when
;  BSS left the image - and 2026-09-08 needs a third, because the autoboot
;  record left the image window too. A core procedure that grows an `If`
;  every time a board gains a region is the wrong shape: the core cannot
;  know what a board reserves, and the Arduino UNO Q's regions are not
;  these.
;
;  So the board enumerates and the core walks. HitsMonitor() is a loop
;  over HwMonRegions() and it does not change again when another region
;  appears; it is written once here, per board, beside the constants it
;  describes.
;
;  THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE. Region 0 is the code, because it is the one
;  an operator hits by aiming a payload at the load address and it is the
;  one whose extent moved.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwMonRegions()
  ProcedureReturn 6
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonRegionLo(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn HwMonLo()
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #MON_DATA_LO
    Case 2 : ProcedureReturn #AB_BASE
    Case 3 : ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_LO
    Case 4 : ProcedureReturn #CORE_RAW_STACK_LO
    Case #MON_REGION_MODULES : ProcedureReturn #MOD_REGION_LO
  EndSelect
  ; A region index this board does not have. Answering an address here
  ; would make HitsMonitor() refuse a range for a region that does not
  ; exist; an empty range (lo above hi) matches nothing and is the only
  ; answer that cannot invent a refusal.
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonRegionHi(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn HwMonHi()
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #MON_DATA_HI
    Case 2 : ProcedureReturn #AB_BASE + #AB_BYTES - 1
    Case 3 : ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_HI
    Case 4 : ProcedureReturn #CORE_RAW_STACK_HI
    Case #MON_REGION_MODULES : ProcedureReturn #MOD_REGION_HI
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; A short phrase naming the region, printed by `map` beside its addresses.
; A printing procedure rather than a returned string for the same reason
; HwIdSayConsole() and I2cSayPins() are: the board owns the prose about
; its own hardware and the core owns the layout of the line.
Procedure HwMonRegionSay(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0
      Print("this monitor's code, measured from the image")
    Case 1
      Print("its variables, placed by --bss-addr")
    Case 2
      Print("the autoboot record")
    Case 3
      Print("the DSI framebuffer")
    Case 4
      Print("the reserved raw-secondary stacks")
    Case #MON_REGION_MODULES
      Print("the driver-module arena and its staging window")
    Default
      Print("a region this board does not have")
  EndSelect
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  HwPay*() - THE PAYLOAD WINDOWS, AND THE LOW ONE FOLLOWS THE IMAGE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE LOW WINDOW'S BOTTOM EDGE IS COMPUTED FROM THE CANONICAL PLACEMENT -
;  #MON_LO plus the size of this image, rounded up to #MON_GRAIN - and NOT
;  from where this copy happens to be running. That distinction is worth
;  the paragraph:
;
;    what the WINDOW says is "here is a band of DRAM a payload may live
;    in", and the band is bounded below by the monitor that will be
;    resident after the next reset, which is the one at #MON_LO.
;
;    what HitsMonitor() says is "that particular range would land on the
;    program that is running right now", and THAT question is answered
;    from HwMonLo()/HwMonHi(), which follow the running image.
;
;  A monitor relocated to $2000000 for a live-bench swap therefore still
;  advertises the same window a payload was built for, still accepts
;  `b 400000`, and now also refuses a load on top of itself - which the
;  old constants got backwards in both halves.
;
;  THE SIZE IS THE ONLY INPUT. There is no ceiling anywhere in this
;  arithmetic: an image of any size produces a window that starts above
;  it. That is the ruling of 2026-09-08 written as code.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwPayWindows()
  ProcedureReturn 2
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPayLo(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn MonGrainUp(#MON_LO + HwMonBytes())
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #PAY1_LO
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPayHi(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn #PAY0_HI
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #PAY1_HI
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwStageAddr() - WHERE A HOST SHOULD PUT A FILE IT IS SENDING
;
;  Every upload tool used to carry 0x400000 as a constant of its own:
;  tools/pi4_upload.py's DEFAULT_ADDR, and STAGE in anvil_update.py,
;  anvil_wifi_update.py and anvil_putfile.py. Four copies of a number
;  this file owns, on the far side of a serial cable, none of which could
;  learn that the monitor had grown. The tools ask for this instead - the
;  same shape as U-Boot's $loadaddr, which a script reads with printenv
;  rather than assuming.
;
;  It is the bottom of the low payload window and nothing more: the first
;  address that is clear of the monitor by construction, in the window
;  every refusal already prints.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwStageAddr()
  ProcedureReturn HwPayLo(0)
EndProcedure
